A well-structured Terraform module has three files at minimum: `main.tf` containing the resource definitions, `variables.tf` defining the inputs, and `outputs.tf` exposing results to callers. Adding `versions.tf` (required provider versions) and `README.md` (usage documentation) makes the module production-ready.

**Variable types** give Terraform's type system real power. A `string` variable carries a single value. A `list(string)` carries multiple values of the same type. A `map(string)` carries key-value pairs. An `object({ ... })` carries a structured group of related values, enabling you to pass complex configuration as a single variable rather than many individual ones. Using `type = object(...)` also means Terraform will validate that the caller provided all required fields with correct types before attempting to create any resources.

**Variable validation blocks** (added in Terraform 0.13) let you enforce constraints at plan time rather than waiting for a cloud API to reject an invalid value. Validating that a `kubernetes_version` matches a known EKS version string, or that a CIDR block is in the correct format, surfaces misconfiguration early.

**The `merge()` function** is the idiomatic way to combine caller-supplied tags with module-level tags. The module enforces that `ManagedBy = "terraform"` is always present on every resource, while the caller can add environment-specific tags. `merge(module_tags, caller_tags)` means caller tags take precedence if there is a conflict.

**Conditional resources** within modules use `count = var.feature_enabled ? 1 : 0`. This is how optional features work: if `var.enable_cluster_autoscaler` is `false`, the IAM policy and role for the cluster autoscaler are not created. The module interface exposes the feature as a boolean, hiding the implementation details.

```hcl
# modules/eks-cluster/main.tf
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = { source = "hashicorp/aws", version = "~> 5.0" }
  }
}

resource "aws_eks_cluster" "this" {
  name     = var.cluster_name
  version  = var.kubernetes_version
  role_arn = aws_iam_role.cluster.arn

  vpc_config {
    subnet_ids = var.subnet_ids
  }

  tags = merge(var.tags, {
    ManagedBy = "terraform"
  })
}

resource "aws_eks_node_group" "this" {
  cluster_name    = aws_eks_cluster.this.name
  node_group_name = "${var.cluster_name}-nodegroup"
  node_role_arn   = aws_iam_role.node.arn
  subnet_ids      = var.subnet_ids

  instance_types = var.node_instance_types

  scaling_config {
    desired_size = var.min_nodes
    min_size     = var.min_nodes
    max_size     = var.max_nodes
  }

  tags = var.tags
}

# modules/eks-cluster/outputs.tf
output "cluster_name" {
  value = aws_eks_cluster.this.name
}

output "cluster_endpoint" {
  value = aws_eks_cluster.this.endpoint
}

output "cluster_ca" {
  value     = aws_eks_cluster.this.certificate_authority[0].data
  sensitive = true
}

# environments/prod/main.tf — calling the module
module "eks_prod" {
  source = "../../modules/eks-cluster"

  cluster_name          = "eks-prod-mach-x7kq"
  kubernetes_version    = "1.35"
  node_instance_types   = ["m6a.2xlarge"]
  min_nodes             = 3
  max_nodes             = 10
  subnet_ids            = module.vpc.private_subnet_ids

  tags = {
    Environment = "prod"
    Team        = "platform"
  }
}

# environments/staging/main.tf
module "eks_staging" {
  source = "../../modules/eks-cluster"

  cluster_name        = "eks-staging"
  kubernetes_version  = "1.35"
  node_instance_types = ["t3.medium"]   # cheaper for staging
  min_nodes           = 2
  max_nodes           = 5
  subnet_ids          = module.vpc.private_subnet_ids

  tags = { Environment = "staging" }
}
```
